content.thinking:
While building, consider the front module power call: `mf->pdata->gpio_cfg` for the 5e3 module. It will enable VDD_VTCAM rails, set MCLK pin to function 0 (but the MCLK clock gate may be off), reset high. Then we force reset. The I2C probe on gpf0-2/3 should work if the front sensor is alive.

But the front sensor's MCLK clock is off; if it needs MCLK for I2C, the probe will fail even if alive. We should enable the front MCLK clock. `fimc_is_sensor_mclk_on` is declared in fimc-is-device-sensor.h; include that header? ischain.c already includes "fimc-is-device-sensor.h"? Check. If not, we can call `exynos_fimc_is_sensor_mclk_on(mf->pdev, 0, 1)` declared in mach/exynos-fimc-is-sensor.h. The module pdata has mclk_on pointer: `mf->pdata` is `exynos_platform_fimc_is_module` (not sensor pdata), which may not have mclk_on. The sensor device's pdata has mclk_on. Use `core->sensor[1].pdata->mclk_on(core->sensor[1].pdev, 0, 1)` and mclk_off. But the state bit may need management.

Alternatively, the MCLK clock might already be on because the front sensor's S_INPUT? In our rear test, no. So enable it.

Let's check `core->sensor[1].pdata->mclk_on` availability. `struct fimc_is_device_sensor` has pdata `exynos_platform_fimc_is_sensor` with mclk_on/off. We can call directly (bypassing the state bit):
```
if (core->sensor[1].pdata->mclk_on) {
  core->sensor[1].pdata->mclk_on(core->sensor[1].pdev, 0, 1);
  udelay(5000);
}
...
if (..mclk_off) mclk_off(...)
```
This sets the rate and enables sclk_isp_sensor1. Good. Add this to the front block. The build is running; we can wait and do a second build, or kill the build, patch, rebuild. Given the user wants both, better to include MCLK now. But editing while make runs risks inconsistent compile. Check if make has already compiled ischain.c; likely early. We can wait ~7min, test without MCLK first, and if front fails, add MCLK.

Maybe the front sensor doesn't need MCLK for I2C; many sensors do, but let's test the simpler version first.

Wait for build.

